  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how nucleic acids and lipids are combined at acidic pH to form different lipid nanoparticles, and how these structures change when the pH is raised. At the top, three preparation routes are sketched: simple stirring of preformed vesicles in ethanol-containing buffer, rapid mixing of lipid dissolved in ethanol with nucleic acid in water, and a microfluidic-style mixer. All involve nucleic acid in water at about pH 4 meeting lipids in ethanol.\n\nThe central drawings show that this acidic mixing first produces compact multilayered particles in which nucleic acid is associated with positively charged lipid membranes. One particle appears mostly single-shell, while another is clearly onion-like, with several concentric lipid bilayers surrounding an aqueous core.\n\nThe lower part indicates what happens after neutralization to around pH 7. The multilamellar precursor can become a more ordered particle with nucleic acid condensed inside an inner compartment, or it can reorganize into a larger, irregular multi-compartment structure with separate internal domains. Overall, the image emphasizes that formulation method and pH-driven reorganization control nanoparticle architecture, from simple vesicles to multilamellar and more complex encapsulating structures.",
